## 3. The six routed blockers

### 3.1 BL-1 — Blast-radius classification. **RC-12 is falsified; the goal's premise holds.**

This was the blocking blocker. `DERIV-L07` RC-12 argued that "zero `kernel/` edits" does not imply
SOFT, resting on two premises, and concluded that the goal is not autonomously sealable and that its
selection rationale over L15/L18 is void.

**Premise 1 — "`constitution/classifier_rules/` is empty, so with a conservative HARD default and
no rules to narrow it, HARD is the expected return."**

**FALSE.** `constitution/classifier_rules/` is indeed empty — `.gitkeep`, 0 bytes **[direct]** — but
the classifier does not read it. `reviews/tasks/B0.11_review.md:39` records the delivered
implementation verbatim: *"**PASS / minimal. No regex, no config**; `posixpath` stdlib + a
top-segment compare."* **[review-record]** The rule, from the same review at :21-23:

> `kernel/` or `constitution/` ⇒ HARD; else SOFT; unresolvable ⇒ HARD (fail-safe / deny-by-default).

The empty rules directory is a real gap in the *specified* design (`spec/PAEOS-8:117` says
`kernel/classifier.py` classifies "from `classifier_rules/`"), but it is causally disconnected from
the classification outcome. RC-12's premise 1 does not reach its conclusion.

**Premise 2 — "Both prior runs were classified HARD, on this goal's path set."**

**TRUE but mis-attributed.** DEBT-0001 and DEBT-0002 both record `Blast radius: HARD` **[direct]**.
But look at what they were classified *over* — the "Implicated paths" line of both files
(`ledger/debt/DEBT-0001.md:12`, `DEBT-0002.md:12`) is identical:

> `backlog/`, `kernel/`, `ledger/`, `operations/`, `reviews/`, `runtime/`,
> `runtime/agents/__init__.py`, `runtime/mitigation.py`, `runtime/orchestrator/__init__.py`,
> `spec/`, `tests/runtime/test_mitigation_layer.py`

That set is **the run's read scopes unioned with its touched files**, and it contains the bare
directory `kernel/`. Feed that to a classifier whose entire rule is "top path segment ∈
{`kernel`, `constitution`} ⇒ HARD" and it returns HARD — deterministically, and **without any
`kernel/` file having been written.** The HARD verdict is an artifact of the *input set*, not of the
change's blast radius. **[inference, from two direct facts]**

**The direct counter-evidence.** The F2 CI gate runs the same TCB prefixes
(`reviews/tasks/B0.11_review.md:30`, `architecture/invariants.json:41`: *"kernel/ or constitution/
diffs route to HARD-LOOP"*). Across `reviews/tasks/`, that gate has classified this goal's exact
change set SOFT, repeatedly and by name **[review-record]**:

| Review | Recorded classification | Change set |
|---|---|---|
| `B1.C_review.md:13` | **F2-SOFT** | `runtime/agents/` — *the file BC-2 edits* |
| `B1-SOFTLOOP_review.md:13` | **F2-SOFT** | `runtime/orchestrator/` — *the file BC-1 edits* |
| `B2.O_review.md` | **F2-SOFT** | `runtime/verification.py` + `runtime/orchestrator/` |
| `phase3-scheduler_review.md` | **F2-SOFT, kernel untouched** | `runtime/scheduler.py` |
| 12 further B2.x/R5.x reviews | **F2-SOFT: `runtime/` + `tests/` only** | — |

**Verdict.** The change set implied by BC-1, BC-2, BC-4 and BC-6 —
`runtime/orchestrator/__init__.py`, `runtime/agents/__init__.py`, `runtime/mitigation.py`,
`tests/runtime/test_mitigation_layer.py`, plus any new `runtime/*` module — has **no top path
segment in `{kernel, constitution}`** and therefore classifies **SOFT**. The goal's decisive
argument for choosing L07 over L15/L18 survives. `DERIV-L07`'s "Forbids" clause *"Proceeding to L08
with BL-1 unanswered (RC-12)"* is discharged.

**Three carried caveats, all cheap:**

- **C1 — the empty change set classifies HARD.** `B0.11_review.md:50` and :70: *"empty change set,
  blank path, tree-escaping (`..`) … all classify HARD (tested)"*. A run that produces no changed
  paths is classified HARD by fail-safe. This composes badly with the DEBT-0001/0002 failure mode
  (a run that produces nothing) and may fully explain both HARD labels without any read-scope
  contamination at all. Either way the conclusion is the same.
- **C2 — the classification depends on what the caller passes.** `B0.11_review.md:60-61`: the
  Phase-2 analyzer *"replaces the body behind the same `classify_paths` signature; the gate wiring
  (extracting changed paths from a request/proposal) is B0.12's composition step."* The one check
  DESIGN must run is therefore **not** "what does `classify_paths` do" (answered) but **"what path
  set does the autonomous driver hand it?"** If it hands the implicated-paths set, this goal will be
  mislabelled HARD again.
- **C3 — a falsifiable prediction for this very run.** This RESEARCH session's read scope includes
  `constitution/`. `constitution` is a HARD prefix. If the implicated-paths hypothesis is right,
  **this goal's run will be labelled HARD**, for the same wrong reason, and a DEBT-0003 will be
  filed saying so. If the run instead completes without a HARD label, the hypothesis is wrong and
  C1 (empty change set) is the operative cause. Either outcome is informative; both leave the SOFT
  verdict on the *change set* intact.

### 3.2 BL-2 — Root cause of DEBT-0001/0002. **Already diagnosed; the system could not see it.**

Both debt entries say only *"To be investigated"* **[direct]**. They do not need to be.

The friction string is verbatim identical to a fully root-caused live-run failure from twelve days
earlier. `reviews/phase3_m3_live_concurrency.md:155` **[direct]**:

> | Both outcomes | REMANDED — *"no evidence submitted to the court for this run"* |
> | Court pool | **empty** — no `submit_evidence` call landed |

and at :169-186, the root cause, named to the line:

> `runtime/integrations/__init__.py:113` `_allowed_tools()` grants only `["Read", "Write", "Edit"]`
> … `acceptEdits` auto-applies **file edits only**; it does **not** auto-approve `Bash`. A headless
> `claude -p` session has no human to approve, so **every command execution is declined** … The
> builders made the **correct** edits … but could not **run** the verification command → nothing
> probative to submit → the court remanded.

Filed as **DEBT-0019**, which `ledger/debt/README.md` indexes as **RESOLVED** **[direct]**.

So the 2026-08-13 recurrence is *not* an unknown. It is one of exactly three things, and the three
are cheaply distinguishable:

1. **A regression of DEBT-0019** — `Bash` was later withdrawn. This is not speculative:
   `ledger/debt/README.md` indexes **DEBT-0021 — "Over-broad tool authority in session tool
   allow-list (`Bash` granted to non-Builder sub-sessions) (RESOLVED)"**. DEBT-0019 granted `Bash`;
   DEBT-0021 took it back from non-Builders. **[direct on both index lines]**
2. **A write-scope drop** — `ledger/debt/README.md` indexes **DEBT-0023 — "Fixed write-scope
   coupling in autonomous intake generation drops valid deliverable files" (OPEN)** **[direct]**.
   This is an *open* defect that silently discards a session's deliverables. It is a direct hazard
   to BC-2's narrow `mitigation/` write scope.
3. **A generic label** — the RB-0008 friction handler may emit this string for *any* non-seal with
   an empty court pool, in which case it is a symptom name, not a diagnosis.

**Why this is the highest-leverage finding for the L07 design, not just for the test.**

DEBT-0019's fix was scoped to *"the IMPLEMENT/VERIFY session"*
(`phase3_m3_live_concurrency.md:196`), and DEBT-0021 then narrowed tool authority to Builders.
**L07 is a PLANNER stage that must submit evidence.** That combination has never been exercised.
The good news is that it does not need `Bash`: per `spec/PAEOS-7.6:237-238` the CAS `put` returns
the hash and `court.submit_evidence` is an MCP method, so a planner can put the catalogue, name a
`reproducible_command`, and submit — the *kernel* re-runs the command (T2), not the agent. The
requirement is therefore about grants, not tools:

> **R-BL2 (binding on DESIGN).** The `_MITIGATION` RoleSpec's `mcp_servers` allow-list MUST include
> `court` and `artifacts`. If the existing planner RoleSpecs (`_RE_DERIVE`, `_IDEATE`, `_TRADEOFF`)
> omit `court`, then **no cognitive-pipeline stage can submit evidence at all**, and that — not
> `Bash` — is the standing cause of "no evidence submitted to the court" on L03–L07 runs. This is a
> one-line check and it is the single most valuable thing DESIGN can do first.

**The discriminator RC-11 demanded.** `DERIV-L07` RC-11 requires the negative test to distinguish
*"the obligation evaluated to false"* from *"the submission path is broken"*, and forbids declaring
criterion 4 satisfied by any remand. M3 supplies the exact mechanical discriminator, already used
once in anger: **inspect the court evidence pool.** Pool empty ⇒ submission path broken (test
invalid, retry). Pool contains the coverage-check evidence *and* the gate denied ⇒ the obligation
discriminated (criterion 4 genuinely satisfied). `reviews/tasks/R5.3_review.md` confirms the pool is
a first-class inspectable object — `FileCourtEvidencePool`, read by `McpWorkerTransport` as an
`EvidenceSource` **[review-record]**. Assert on the pool, not on `outcome != COMMITTED`.

